  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 AM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: Midlevel longwave troughing currently over Canada \ncontinues to expand southward and push cooler cP air into the \nnorthwoods this weekend. At the same time, surface low pressure over \nthe Central U.S. with an attached cold front will sweep across the \nCWA and produce another round of light rainfall. \n\nPost-frontal cold air advection begins to move into the region this \nFriday and return surface temperatures to climatological normals for \nearly October. Highs return to the 60s for most areas while \novernight lows fall into the 40s and 30s. Seasonably cold overnight \ntemperatures are expected at times, especially Friday night as \ntemperatures fall around freezing for several counties. Only chance \nof rainfall beyond today remains on Sunday as an embedded wave \nsweeps across Southern Ontario. Otherwise high pressure will support \ndry conditions through early next week. \n\nForecast Details:  \n\nToday: The aforementioned cold front continues to push across \nNorthern Michigan today, resulting in another round of light \nrainfall for most areas. Rainfall is expected to remain confined to \nNorthern Lower with most areas observing only a general tenth of \nlight stratiform rain. Stronger forcing and frontogenic lift will \nstay across the southernmost parts of the CWA, with deeper moisture \nprofiles. As a result, a sharp gradient of higher QPF amounts around \nan inch is expected along and south of the M-55 corridor. \n\nFriday and Saturday: Surface high pressure will begin to build \nbehind the cold fronts passing tonight. Post-frontal cold air \nadvection will usher in cool and dry cP air into the northwoods with \nhighs in the 60s and breezy northwest flow this Friday. Only \nconcerning weather to keep an eye on will be the chilly overnight \ntemperatures Friday night. Efficient radiational cooling from clear \nskies and breezy northwest flow will drop temperatures to the low \n30s for most areas. \n\nSunday through Wednesday Outlook: An embedded trough will develop \nover the Canadian Rockies this weekend and return some isolated \nchances of showers to Northern Michigan this Sunday. With the most \nforcing to our north, the majority of rainfall will be limited to \nthe eastern upper with highest probabilities of QPF remaining below \na quarter inch. The remainder of the forecast period will remain \npretty benign with fall-like conditions. Partly to mostly cloudy \nskies with highs in the 60s continue through Wednesday with \novernight lows in the 40s. The typically colder parts of the CWA can \nexpect scattered chances of frost concerns next week as we continue \nour late growing season.",
